 of the following vector spaces has a dimension of $36?$\n\n- ${R}_{6}[t]$, the vector space of all polynomials in the variable $t$ of degree at most $6$\n- ${\\text{M}}_{12\\times 3}(R),$ the vector space of all $12\\times 3$ matrices over $R$\n- ${R}^{36}$" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of the vector spaces in turn.\n\n- Recall that $\\text{dim}({R}_{n}[t])=n+1.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({R}_{6}[t])=6+1=7\\ne 36.\\,\\times \n$$\n- Recall that $\\text{dim}({\\text{M}}_{n\\times m}(R))=n\\cdot m.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({\\text{M}}_{12\\times 3}(R))=12\\cdot 3=36.\\,✓\n$$\n- Recall that $\\text{dim}({R}^{n})=n.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({R}^{36})=36.\\,✓\n$$\n\nHence, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-95230/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "aee17e3a-031d-5c0e-9488-17410b3a2372"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-95230"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-106916/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" :answer/id #uuid "574c1ae6-71c3-5484-b24f-d5ce733dc732" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-106916/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" :answer/id #uuid "a002214a-87b7-5306-a09e-e542c73063d3" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-106916/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" :answer/id #uuid "e50e90bc-0136-594f-8cf2-34d8ad575992" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-106916/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" :answer/id #uuid "c6e8e873-add7-5433-ac6a-f655eca008b3" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-106916/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" :answer/id #uuid "91057ccf-b96f-549d-8973-06280574b0f0" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-106916/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "04c0f25e-9b8c-51e2-91cf-86742a4cd75b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-106916/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" :answer-field/choices ["q-106916/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" "q-106916/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" "q-106916/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" "q-106916/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" "q-106916/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-106916" :question/id #uuid "a94211ad-c433-579f-8c24-254aede14da5" :question/math-academy-id "q-106916" :question/problem "Which of the following vector spaces are finite-dimensional?\n\n- ${ℓ}^{1},$ the vector space of infinite real sequences where the sum of the absolute values of the terms are finite\n- ${R}_{3000}[t],$ the vector space of all polynomials in the variable $t$ of degree at most $3\\,000$\n- ${\\text{M}}_{50\\times 60}(R),$ the vector space of all $50\\times 60$ matrices over $R$" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine our vector spaces.\n\n- ${ℓ}^{1}$ is *not* finite-dimensional. To understand why, we first note that the following subset of ${ℓ}^{1}$ is linearly independent: \n\n$$\n{{e}_{1},\\,{e}_{2},\\,…,{e}_{n}},\\,n\\in N\n$$\n\nwhere, for each $j\\in {1,2,…,n},$\n\n$$\n{e}_{j}=(0,…,0,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{1,}_{{↑}_{{j}^{\\text{th}}\\text{ position}}}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,0,…).\n$$\n\nIndeed, we have that \n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n}{x}_{i}{e}_{i}=0\n$$\n\nif and only if ${x}_{i}=0$ for all $i=0,1,2,…,n.$ Therefore,  for any  $n\\in N,$  there exists a linearly independent subset of ${ℓ}^{1}$ of size $n,$ which imples that  ${ℓ}^{1}$ cannot have finite dimension.\n\n- ${R}_{3000}[t]$ and  ${\\text{M}}_{50\\times 60}(R)$ are finite-dimensional. Indeed,\n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({R}_{3000}[t])=3\\,000+1=3\\,001,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({\\text{M}}_{50\\times 60}(R))=50\\cdot 60=3\\,000\n$$\n\n \nare both finite numbers.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-106916/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e609aabf-7e7e-5997-b8dc-c66e7a14a36f"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-106916"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-95251/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" :answer/id #uuid "96328657-2f4f-5e4f-adb0-431520404f47" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-95251/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" :answer/id #uuid "b4d10777-90b5-50c6-9489-2e40539a1581" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-95251/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" :answer/id #uuid "503f44d5-ea1d-5db1-8bcb-203193ab85a0" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-95251/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" :answer/id #uuid "37b23975-5716-50bd-bc36-962e44b0fae1" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-95251/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" :answer/id #uuid "cbfccd02-7c00-5e95-8003-84a36c86153d" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :d